<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Plain HTTP is allowed, deliberately.

  From Android 9 onward cleartext is blocked by default, and that default is
  right for an app that talks to the public internet. This one does not. It
  talks to exactly one address: a self-hosted console on a LAN, whose address
  it is told at upload time by scanning a QR code. Those deployments run on
  http://<something>:8080 far more often than not — there is no certificate
  authority on a field network, and a self-signed certificate would mean
  either pinning something the app cannot know at build time or teaching
  analysts to tap through a trust warning, which is worse than cleartext.

  What bounds the exposure:

    - The app makes no other network calls. No analytics, no crash reporter,
      no update check. The only host it ever contacts is the one the analyst
      scanned a code for, seconds earlier, standing in front of it.
    - The token it sends is write-only. Somebody reading the traffic learns
      the console's address and gains the ability to post a field report into
      a queue a human then reviews. They cannot read the case file with it.
    - What is actually sensitive — the reports themselves — is encrypted on
      the handset, and in transit is only as exposed as the network the team
      chose to run the console on.

  If you do put the console behind TLS, tighten this: replace the base config
  with a domain-config naming your host and set cleartextTrafficPermitted to
  false. The app needs no change to work over https, only this file.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <!-- A certificate an admin installed on the handset counts too,
                 so a team that does run an internal CA can use it without
                 rebuilding the app. -->
            <certificates src="user" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
